  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the child, including a progressively worsening cough with spells and a characteristic whooping sound, along with a high leukocyte count, strongly suggests pertussis. Pertussis, or whooping cough, is a highly contagious bacterial infection caused by Bordetella pertussis. The presence of a newborn in the household increases the urgency of managing this condition due to the high risk of severe complications in infants. Azithromycin is the preferred antibiotic for pertussis as it is effective against Bordetella pertussis and is suitable for all ages, including newborns. Administering oral azithromycin to all family members can help prevent the spread of the infection. The father's unknown vaccination status and the potential waning immunity of the mother, who was last vaccinated 11 years ago, justify the administration of the Tdap vaccine to both parents to bolster protection against pertussis. This approach aligns with medical consensus on managing pertussis exposure and protecting vulnerable populations, such as infants, from severe outcomes."
